ome, and cite the three tests that already prove it\"\n36\tworkstream = \"process\"\n37\tsafety_class = \"spine\"\n38\tbuildtier = \"strong\"\n39\tpriority = 2\n40\tspecref = \"docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml\"\n41\tbar = \"DevStg-Tests\"\n42\t```\n43\t\n44\tVERDICT THIS CONTINUES:\n45\t`docs/reviews/wi-590-adjudicate-llr-207-llr-208/004-ADJUDICATE-774ef35.md`,\n46\tgoverning line `OUTCOME: RETURN rows=4`. `LLR-208` and `TC-206` were APPROVED by\n47\tthat act and are NOT in this scope. `LLR-207` and `TC-205` return together\n48\tbecause the requirement half and the test half of one gap are the same gap seen\n49\tfrom two sides, and because `staged_drafted_rows` queues an approver only for\n50\trows a delta actually amends — a successor that edited `TC-205` alone would\n51\tleave `LLR-207` with no queued approver.\n52\t\n53\tThis return does NOT inherit WI-586's findings. All of them were re-driven on\n54\tthis tree and all are DISCHARGED: the `governing_identity` HEAD-vs-branch-tip\n55\tclause and the peel-terminus clause now read correctly; every one of `TC-205`'s\n56\t46 citations resolves to an existing, passing test in the file it names; the\n57\t`test_integrate_station` module is cited; the identity-fixture `Method`\n58\tmisstatement is gone; `CMP-006.notes` now names `kitlib/verdict.py (LLR-207)`\n59\tas CMP-008 with `IF-175` as its declared seam; and the `TC-206` trunk-wiring\n60\tgap is closed by a real detector. The finding below is NEW, and it entered the\n61\ttree AFTER the row text was last written: `f4ca1bd5` (\"four batch-lane\n62\tdefects\", merged at `c590637d`) added a second disposable-commit class to the\n63\tgoverning walk; `LLR-207.detail` was last edited at `64692ddf`.\n64\t\n65\tIN SCOPE — two cells, no new mechanism, no regression to write.\n66\t\n67\t1. `LLR-207.detail`, the `governing_rev` clause. It reads \"peeling any verified\n68\t   refresh it meets to reach one those commits would otherwise hide\". The walk\n69\t   does n